src/test/stress/README

Stress tests for CONCURRENTLY commands
======================================

This directory stresses the commands that are meant to behave
transparently to other sessions: the CONCURRENTLY variants of REPACK,
CREATE INDEX, REINDEX, DROP INDEX, REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW and ALTER
TABLE ... DETACH PARTITION, together with the machinery they lean on --
logical decoding, replication slots, the visibility map, the plan cache.

Each test runs a pgbench workload whose clients maintain an invariant
while one of those commands runs against the same tables, and checks
that invariant along with the resulting indexes.  Any SQL error or
broken invariant makes pgbench abort (--exit-on-abort), failing the
test.  The tests are not deterministic: they rely on timing and
randomized workloads, so a passing run does not prove the absence of
bugs.  They are cheap enough to run by default, and can be scaled up.

How a test is built
===================

A test is a scenario: a named combination of plugins.  Each test file
under t/ contains the whole of the scenario it runs and nothing else, so
what a test does can be read from the file that names it.  The plugins
it names live in t/Stress/Plugins.pm and come in six kinds:

  schema    the pgbench schema, plus decorators that alter it: a column
            with an invariant of its own, a constraint, a partitioning
            of one of its tables
  indexes   what is built on them, across every access method
  load      what changes the data while preserving those invariants
  ddl       what runs concurrently with that
  check     what must hold regardless, during and after the run
  env       what the cluster looks like: standalone, a hot standby, a
            subscriber, a cluster being killed and restarted, and so on

The scenario names entries from each; t/Stress/Run.pm documents the
fields, validates the combination, builds the cluster, writes one
pgbench script per load and per check plus one for the DDL rotation, and
runs them as a weighted mix.  Adding a dimension means adding a plugin,
not copying a file; adding a scenario means adding one file that names
plugins that already exist.

See DESIGN for why it is built this way, PORTING for where the coverage
of each of the older hand-written tests ended up -- including what is
not covered to the same depth -- and REGRESSIONS for the historical bugs
the suite is meant to catch and whether it has been shown to.

Duration scaling and reproducing a failure
==========================================

The run time of each test is controlled by the stress_concurrently value
in PG_TEST_EXTRA:

    make check PG_TEST_EXTRA='stress_concurrently=10'

Each scenario is calibrated so that a value of 1 makes it run for about
six seconds, and the increase should be roughly linear; a value of 0
disables the suite.  If PG_TEST_EXTRA does not mention
stress_concurrently at all, a value of 1 is assumed, except in
environments that declare themselves slow by setting
PG_TEST_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT (such as Valgrind buildfarm animals), where the
suite is skipped.

Every run reports the seed it used:

    # stress_seed is 1336750692

Passing it back reproduces the same choices, both in Perl and in
pgbench:

    make check PG_TEST_EXTRA='stress_seed=1336750692'

Soak mode (see t/Stress/Soak.pm) numbers every combination it runs --
only those, since combinations whose pieces do not fit are discarded
before they are numbered -- and which combination a number names depends
only on the seed, so a failure reported as invented_57 comes back
without repeating the hour before it:

    make check PG_TEST_EXTRA='stress_mode=soak stress_seed=1336750692
                              stress_soak_skip=57 stress_soak_count=1'

The same two settings split a long soak into shorter runs that add up to
it; each run reports the index to resume from.

The generated pgbench scripts are written into the test's data directory
and echoed into its log, and the pgbench command line is logged too, so
a failing scenario can be replayed by hand.  Every node also runs with
verbose errors, backtraces for relation_open and log_lock_waits on:
these produce no output unless something goes wrong, and a failure that
arrives without its call site is expensive to chase.

Chaos
=====

Most of the windows these tests hunt are microseconds wide.  Repetition
does not find those: a scenario can run for hours without ever placing
two operations within a microsecond of each other.  What finds them is
widening the window, for a small fraction of the operations that reach
it, until it is wide enough to lose.

A chaos profile does that.  It attaches jitter -- a sleep of a given
length, taken with a given probability -- to injection points in the
paths these races live in, and can turn on the probabilistic form of
debug_discard_caches for the cache-flush hazards.  The profiles are in
t/Stress/Plugins.pm under %CHAOS, and a scenario asks for one with

  chaos => 'light',

which is orthogonal to its environment.

The points worth jittering are listed in %CHAOS_POINTS, each with a cap
on how likely and how long its sleep may be.  The caps are not decoration:
a point every commit reaches has to stay rare, a point in a lock queue
has to stay short, and a phase change in a concurrent build that happens
a few times a run can be slept on every time.

Soak does not pick from the named profiles.  It invents one -- a few
points from the pool, each drawn inside its caps -- because there are
far more combinations of points than anyone would write down and the
interesting ones are pairs nobody thought to pair.  Most combinations
get none, so that a failure under chaos can still be told from one that
does not need it.  The invented profile is printed with the rest of the
combination and comes back with the seed:

  chaos=[commit-before-clog-update:0.06992/465-11382] discard=0.00044

Two properties are what make a chaos failure worth reporting, and both
are deliberate.  A profile only ever delays: nothing the server decides
changes because of it, so a failure seen under chaos is a failure that
exists without it.  And no sleep approaches lock_timeout -- a lock
request that dawdles at the head of a queue stalls every writer behind
it, which ends a run in a cascade that tested nothing.

Every run reports what the jitter did:

  # chaos 'decoding': 649 sleeps totalling 26078119us

because a run where nothing slept tested no more than a run without
chaos, and the two are otherwise indistinguishable from the outside.

A build without injection points ignores all of it, with a note.

Reproducers for open bugs
=========================

A scenario tagged 'open-bug' reproduces a bug that has not been fixed
yet, so it fails against master on purpose.  Those are skipped unless

  PG_TEST_EXTRA='stress_open_bugs=1'

which keeps the suite green for the failures nobody expects, and keeps
soak from walking into one.  When the fix lands, the tag comes off.

Running the tests
=================

NOTE: You must have given the --enable-tap-tests argument to configure.

Run
    make check
or
    make installcheck
You can use "make installcheck" if you previously did "make install".
In that case, the code in the installation tree is tested.  With
"make check", a temporary installation tree is built from the current
sources and then tested.

See src/test/perl/README for more info about running these tests.
